#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348219 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ad1b940-29ef-5bb3-ab76-b9022d36ef57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{36}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345436} {:answer/id #uuid "5bab3d83-01ac-56d9-87c7-89af73d69a22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{6}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{36}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346134} {:answer/id #uuid "7b0ebee1-e81a-5755-a697-cbac27922e86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{18}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346426} {:answer/id #uuid "7d3731bc-0af6-503e-8640-17df73965f48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{36}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346446} {:answer/id #uuid "bb7fa320-1feb-5ea9-b5c0-8430abb685cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y-5)^{2}}{6}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346976}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346446} :answer-field/id #uuid "21a04564-f6ec-5bf2-ab6f-b6795ab6636b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347662}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "9015a8d2-7682-57fa-9d91-2827376ba1a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-79377" :question/problem "Determine the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -5,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -5 \\\\ \\frac{x+5}{3} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{9} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2 \\\\ \\frac{y+2}{6} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{36} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{36} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x+5)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{36}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351189 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f3db826-20ca-540b-9b96-6dc6ed206e9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348865} {:answer/id #uuid "43f3642f-8a9e-5fee-a6d8-eabf284c8f26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349065} {:answer/id #uuid "47e75774-fac8-5062-880a-727a16ba9eb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349096} {:answer/id #uuid "7ecbe21c-5609-5fa1-ba0f-81eb95a13511" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349608} {:answer/id #uuid "ae441ea5-b6f0-508a-83ed-e409f76a2a51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349987}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349987} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf326210-69ad-557c-8a58-7a89edc90759" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351042}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fd7813dc-b09d-5c4d-8539-6d09e01430cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-80983" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the graph $y=\\sqrt{x}$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to point $B(1,1),$ then  $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}+y)\\,dx+(3x+2y)\\,dy=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y=\\sqrt{x}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j={t}^{2}\\,i+t\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=2t\\,i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}+y)\\,dx+(3x+2y)\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+(3x+2y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(({x}^{2}+y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+(3x+2y)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({({t}^{2})}^{2}+t)\\cdot 2t+(3{t}^{2}+2t)\\cdot 1\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{4}+t)\\cdot 2t+3{t}^{2}+2t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2{t}^{5}+5{t}^{2}+2t\\,dt \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{6}}{3}+\\frac{5{t}^{3}}{3}+{t}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{(1)^{6}}{3}+\\frac{5(1)^{3}}{3}+(1)^{2}]-[0] \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{5}{3}+1]-[0] \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351216 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17becf89-3701-509a-a9a8-440484064ed9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348648} {:answer/id #uuid "20742a72-7769-5476-9ce9-3b1881e0470b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348727} {:answer/id #uuid "47810cbc-2cf1-5cf8-8896-58252baa012e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349094} {:answer/id #uuid "ee29d359-0faa-5f3d-aacd-2a4fd062f399" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350529} {:answer/id #uuid "f83e5f36-bde0-573a-91de-bf6ecdf50c44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350622}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349094} :answer-field/id #uuid "81d0252a-6745-5c15-b97f-a22408b67a5b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350931}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7a071a2f-be85-5b88-9507-97c63159bbb0" :question/math-academy-id "q-81098" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the parabola $y={x}^{2}-x$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to point $B(2,2),$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}2xy\\,dx+x\\,dy=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y={x}^{2}-x.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=t\\,i+({t}^{2}-t)\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,2]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=i+(2t-1)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}2xy\\,dx+x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2xy\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(2xy\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2t({t}^{2}-t)\\cdot 1+t(2t-1)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2{t}^{3}-t\\,dt \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{4}}{2}-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{{2}^{4}}{2}-\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}]-[0] \\\\  & =8-2 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351218 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "499b11ec-b714-5e35-93a8-a00dd90bd487" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349115} {:answer/id #uuid "4b3d1df7-d187-5a76-9ff2-d586354e93f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349135} {:answer/id #uuid "a07e3936-f719-59ae-bec5-b46ca80359cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349872} {:answer/id #uuid "ce5f8cad-b0d3-57e8-8823-669fc1f82f6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350261} {:answer/id #uuid "dc675675-601c-5a06-9c0d-83e3763e65c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350373}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349135} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a5f8fc5-bd8c-52a6-b3a1-caea03d05cdf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350917}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "88864edb-1c9c-588d-bdc6-1eef5e251f66" :question/math-academy-id "q-81102" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from the point $A(0,1)$ to the point $B(2,3),$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}{e}^{x}\\,dx-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi y}{2}\\right)\\,dy=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=j$ and $b=2\\,i+3\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =j+t(2\\,i+3\\,j-j) \\\\  & =j+t(2\\,i+2j) \\\\  & =2t\\,i+(1+2t)\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x(t)=2t$ and $y(t)=1+2t$ along the segment.\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=2\\,i+2\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{e}^{x}\\,dx-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi y}{2}\\right)\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{x}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi y}{2}\\right)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({e}^{x}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi y}{2}\\right)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{2t}\\cdot 2-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi (1+2t)}{2}\\right)\\cdot 2\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{e}^{2t}-2\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi t))dt \\\\  & ={[{e}^{2t}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi t)]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[{e}^{2(1)}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi (1))]-[{e}^{2(0)}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi (0))] \\\\  & =[{e}^{2}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)]-[1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)] \\\\  & =[{e}^{2}+0]-[1+0] \\\\  & ={e}^{2}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351250 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5480a9aa-ad9e-5901-99ef-a4b557010682" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2+6\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349219} {:answer/id #uuid "9515cda8-658c-596e-82bc-d1176ae74422" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2+10\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349782} {:answer/id #uuid "d708ba15-3640-5e0f-a2cc-8efebb555009" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+12\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350330} {:answer/id #uuid "e9a9cd99-2cbe-59b5-8edc-4b78ef09dc96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+14\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350495} {:answer/id #uuid "f55e6f64-fa6a-5856-9b77-a53e676b505a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+16\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350593}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350495} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ed4da5c-67f9-5122-bb2a-f6a68757af78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350790}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "bbe13972-64bc-5d76-9613-e21318b0221e" :question/math-academy-id "q-81180" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the parabola $y=\\pi {x}^{2}$ traversed from the point $A(1,\\pi )$ to point $B(2,4\\pi ),$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{2{x}^{2}}\\right)\\,dx+3x\\,dy=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y=\\pi {x}^{2}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=t\\,i+\\pi {t}^{2}\\,j,\\,t\\in [1,2]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=i+2\\pi t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{2{x}^{2}}\\right)\\,dx+3x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{2{x}^{2}}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+3x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{2{x}^{2}}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+3x\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi {t}^{2}}{2{t}^{2}}\\right)\\cdot 1+3t\\cdot 2\\pi t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+6\\pi {t}^{2}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}1+6\\pi {t}^{2}\\,dt \\\\  & =[t+2\\pi {t}^{3}]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =[2+2\\pi (2)^{3}]-[1+2\\pi (1)^{3}] \\\\  & =[2+16\\pi ]-[1+2\\pi ] \\\\  & =1+14\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351278 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "062ad924-b9ac-5458-b29b-3d01c466d8d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348501} {:answer/id #uuid "0d89f367-4443-5fe8-8644-145ec0dab50b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348565} {:answer/id #uuid "3d2f2b26-f487-53ac-904f-278604ef6f78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349000} {:answer/id #uuid "96d294c8-f380-5d82-a452-5c76194ff827" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349799} {:answer/id #uuid "fa9c4eab-46e0-5668-a2bc-a3d8dd51f5f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{cos}^{2}⁡(u+v){sin}^{2}⁡(u-v)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350651}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348565} :answer-field/id #uuid "03915e0d-dd82-5c3f-80f0-7fc6e5e74eb6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350711}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3a033a41-4f6f-52b1-9278-922a7269908f" :question/math-academy-id "q-81252" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $$\n\\displaystyle T(\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, we have $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)$ and $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v).$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v) & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)\\, & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354514 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f1b3428-f0ff-5ea9-ae99-bf8657ba7b52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2+{e}^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352412} {:answer/id #uuid "689b5ddb-7080-5cde-993b-3f0536812305" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352500} {:answer/id #uuid "7cdee458-66d1-5075-a5b3-b90c8837d4a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2+e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352664} {:answer/id #uuid "bb7a10a6-a680-5c5d-bddf-8913d3cf78ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353158} {:answer/id #uuid "d4fc7604-4fda-5395-8026-a179d600a128" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1-{e}^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353415}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186352412} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7aa88dc-8c66-5720-9d75-ddad96eeb69d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354161}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ce4a1a73-6331-5e40-b163-83a2f690e9c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-86812" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)={z}^{2}{e}^{xy}-{y}^{2}{e}^{yz},$ evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}$ at the point $(0,-1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $z,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the sum and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({z}^{2}{e}^{xy}-{y}^{2}{e}^{yz}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({z}^{2}{e}^{xy})-\\frac{∂}{∂z}({y}^{2}{e}^{yz}) \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂z}({z}^{2})-{y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{yz})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{xy}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂z}({z}^{2})-{y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{yz}) & ={e}^{xy}\\cdot 2z-{y}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{yz}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂z}(yz) \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}\\cdot 2z-{y}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{yz}\\cdot y \\\\  & =2z{e}^{xy}-{y}^{3}{e}^{yz}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(0,-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂z}{|}_{(0,-1,1)} & =2(1){e}^{(0)(-1)}-(-1)^{3}{e}^{(-1)(1)} \\\\  & =2+{e}^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357487 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be1f2782-b94c-5338-8044-608f3468b85f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{40}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356328} {:answer/id #uuid "cb6d80f7-dfb7-512a-abad-766ac81adffe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356439} {:answer/id #uuid "d5012490-41ad-5072-9d60-53f86f6f37ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356527} {:answer/id #uuid "d7358768-6a5a-5876-8e36-c2f97c5a588a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356546} {:answer/id #uuid "e6036eac-887e-5728-bc69-99a208e6785a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356673}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356439} :answer-field/id #uuid "1281a916-cca9-5586-a7c5-f8adff0691eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356950}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d77bf889-9285-57c2-9d70-8b359a57d0fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-90848" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the parabola $y={x}^{2}-1$ traversed from the point $A(0,-1)$ to point $B(2,3),$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}5{x}^{2}y\\,dx+{x}^{2}(y+1)\\,dy=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y={x}^{2}-1.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=t\\,i+({t}^{2}-1)\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,2]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=i+2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}5{x}^{2}y\\,dx+{x}^{2}(y+1)\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}5{x}^{2}y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+{x}^{2}(y+1)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(5{x}^{2}y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+{x}^{2}(y+1)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}5{t}^{2}({t}^{2}-1)\\cdot 1+{t}^{2}({t}^{2}-1+1)\\cdot 2t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}5{t}^{4}-5{t}^{2}+2{t}^{5}\\,dt \\\\  & ={[{t}^{5}-\\frac{5}{3}{t}^{3}+\\frac{{t}^{6}}{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =[{2}^{5}-\\frac{5}{3}(2)^{3}+\\frac{{2}^{6}}{3}]-[0] \\\\  & =32-\\frac{40}{3}+\\frac{64}{3} \\\\  & =40\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357488 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b5a7777-a54c-5144-9dd2-6db2bdfadf58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355029} {:answer/id #uuid "61232685-a7a4-54c0-b2f7-04ab7b7daab9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355527} {:answer/id #uuid "9e198969-77e7-54c7-a4d5-8fcaa9d1d45a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356073} {:answer/id #uuid "a4d4dda7-ea67-59f3-a16d-acadacf7c5e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356127} {:answer/id #uuid "aaae3ce5-d578-5c1b-a2fb-4ead0dd1fc64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\pi +3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356175}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355527} :answer-field/id #uuid "331df704-6bf7-5c71-9966-44b1422cc8d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356999}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "067b683a-f3df-5f66-b795-e612d7f9ac62" :question/math-academy-id "q-90871" :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the parabola $y=\\pi {x}^{2}$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to point $B(1,\\pi ),$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\right)\\,dx+4{x}^{2}\\,dy=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our curve is given by the explicit Cartesian equation $y=\\pi {x}^{2}.$ As a result, we obtain the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=t\\,i+\\pi {t}^{2}\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=i+2\\pi t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\right)\\,dx+4{x}^{2}\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+4{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\right)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+4{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi {t}^{2}}{{t}^{2}}\\right)\\cdot 1+4{t}^{2}\\cdot 2\\pi t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +8\\pi {t}^{3}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}8\\pi {t}^{3}-1\\,dt \\\\  & =[2\\pi {t}^{4}-t]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[2\\pi (1)^{4}-1]-[0] \\\\  & =2\\pi -1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362296 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13075448-9332-5a8a-a396-e4860890d643" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/fe/fee6d2843a1f59af1283aff4fceaaa453636d05895994a65c8e6f513fee8a4d6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360912} {:answer/id #uuid "1589e2e4-f18d-5ece-b14a-46c4e2508450" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/b0/b0a411f453d3127592077108cfb56d2b295a2fb856acf5dcca3304ccc3b0756d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360921} {:answer/id #uuid "29ca04ae-da0c-5051-b66f-a8077fe37711" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/ba/ba346990bd792f1e4a9aed0d900ca087315544715a35cd5cd90049c99fdb84ba.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361023} {:answer/id #uuid "35d8329f-d338-563b-96c0-e698e3b6c5f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/75/754c4ffbf7675b321d4a7377c134486fb142b6764a885134e5a4088ef759b295.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361076} {:answer/id #uuid "52eb6055-c112-58fc-b2a8-05a9c5443a1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/08/083e8bce54a03c96d32fe2387c1fcac094c9b537f88e486dc8e523003c44412a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361205}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361023} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ba20f59-5c67-5419-977a-448b36b1f5cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362126}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f3384be2-f55b-58d3-ab9f-9385bb18dd09" :question/math-academy-id "q-98395" :question/problem "Which of the following regions is connected?" :question/worked-solution "A region $D$ is connected if every pair of points in $D$ can be joined by a smooth curve or a path in $D.$\n\nAmong the given options, the only one that satisfies this definition is the following:\n\n![](images/ba/ba346990bd792f1e4a9aed0d900ca087315544715a35cd5cd90049c99fdb84ba.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362309 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65bd1623-744f-536f-84f9-ddb85be2f34c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/1a/1ae143077958b5c8349d94cd80cae6f21f4327220cc3c0f50d6f13fcb3518488.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361297} {:answer/id #uuid "96f58719-61f6-5190-81c5-abc11151ac43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/69/69162c231a9a3d51494afb4ab0fee60e48417e5c30c07ee9e4a9d6cdfbc12032.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361511} {:answer/id #uuid "9cf53998-1583-5dcf-b4a5-bd1367499cb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/c8/c8842f5b37ad7ae0a38ecdcdb972006f3ea13a4fc1bbddffa17e99469213b19f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361536} {:answer/id #uuid "c490daf2-7c83-5c7f-918c-0b2df099cbb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/a7/a713209bebb01be8158c5363392fe13b6b6ebb7f3312f891ea14e4dc6d3fa97c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361722} {:answer/id #uuid "e24c9e7a-f0dc-5461-aa37-5cb8c52a54eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/84/848cf1a33161ac9fb25282cadc2f469675198a69b1df4a04d5eb88c15f66ebcc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361850}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361850} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e1db278-b5db-5555-8457-4e01c99ea370" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362076}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d07e9501-4562-5f84-9934-b4d893bcda8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-98682" :question/problem "Which of the following is *not* a simply connected region?" :question/worked-solution "A connected region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ can be contracted to a point.\n\n![](images/46/46305c070e2563b4dacf8b31c88c46b860f003b20ffdff608a5485c09a91bd11.png)\n\nA connected *plane* region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ encloses only points in $D.$ In other words, the region does not contain any holes. Plane regions containing holes are not simply connected, since some simple closed curves cannot be contracted to a point:\n\n![](images/a0/a0459ddbabbd810e43db084f5dd174437340914b53ed227f6ef7ce2e3a0fc383.png)\n\nAmong the given options, the only one that does not satisfy this definition is the following:  \n\n![](images/84/848cf1a33161ac9fb25282cadc2f469675198a69b1df4a04d5eb88c15f66ebcc.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362310 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fffacc9e-beec-5b87-a6ad-4054725f30a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469934} {:answer/id #uuid "5a491a09-f7b0-54c0-8c16-31aefd05fbc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469935}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186469935} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc494cca-9c27-5636-819c-d7ba48ac2365" :answer-field/key "![Region I](images/f3/f362e380a6641d2b82e55d782a56c2514301c87f2dc1c6e617e9903d6f36c6e3.svg)" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470579} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c593b10-83f5-57ab-b0fa-86ecb233c7ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469936} {:answer/id #uuid "4172b6dc-4962-502e-91c0-4be1831ffab0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469937}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186469937} :answer-field/id #uuid "e392a014-4c1c-5154-b560-d0b81245c899" :answer-field/key "![Region II](images/de/deba611fa0a44adbf397bb20ac53dfcac4e0543f9c02039e9b329c0a383ab4b2.svg)" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470580} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "771cd02f-fda7-5ee6-af63-ec59da5fabdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469938} {:answer/id #uuid "765b46eb-b850-5e76-81ad-42e64146ea0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469939}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186469939} :answer-field/id #uuid "648a2bf6-5faa-57ac-a014-76cffeaf60dc" :answer-field/key "![Region III](images/8a/8a72d04c8d0257fe5c02e11ac9487da01e5388c4dde84e86dd2bdd250f3d94e2.svg)" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470581} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b01d9637-3916-5456-83d5-9644992bb85b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469940} {:answer/id #uuid "950b233d-c663-5478-b3de-db1d19d6b842" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469941}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186469940} :answer-field/id #uuid "84cbe606-0544-50d5-b199-a029d91a2906" :answer-field/key "![Region IV](images/67/67ed89a89d03aa102dbbd75a6286739a0ecba4874684acfcf0a9d819b0a8754d.svg)" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470582}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0317a51a-f6d5-582a-aa69-b3f89cc6775d" :question/math-academy-id "q-98690" :question/problem "Which of the regions shown below are simply connected?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A connected region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ can be contracted to a point.\n\n![](images/7d/7d6aea1a62fb290702630ef95ec86f4119e7c8fdd74b8148b62b66e5ac871b58.png)\n\nA connected *plane* region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ encloses only points in $D.$ In other words, the region does not contain any holes. Plane regions containing holes are not simply connected, since some simple closed curves cannot be contracted to a point:\n\n![](images/c3/c3c3e7a60ff0dd917cf6c0b293dae7510e457a0455629add1c54cadac65248b7.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the regions:  \n\n- Region I is simply connected. This is a connected region that does not contain holes.\n\n- Region II is simply connected. This is a connected region that does not contain holes.\n\n- Region III is simply connected. This is a connected region that does not contain holes.\n\n- Region IV is not simply connected. Instead, this is a connected region that contains a hole.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is regions I, II and III only"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362340 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37ac7900-ea05-5cc8-9d53-11c30c4ca60a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440029} {:answer/id #uuid "91747f90-b1ca-5a23-b764-3011370995c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440030}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440029} :answer-field/id #uuid "536c4571-b3d8-5ac6-b419-3f6f71056077" :answer-field/key "$k=-3$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440372} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1e4cb25-b3e5-5104-8ec6-1f86c12d56d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440031} {:answer/id #uuid "7ff679bc-fdfb-5669-bd8b-854d2358190c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440032}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440032} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5c431c2-1fb0-5a03-b48d-74276cb322b4" :answer-field/key "$k=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440373} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9399f58c-b270-5d89-8f0f-c6e1b2ae15a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440033} {:answer/id #uuid "7cd73f80-9ac4-5715-aa47-0376b208e6f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440034}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440034} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7c8be4f-cbe0-55fc-a506-530979c94394" :answer-field/key "$k=3$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186440374}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e554c090-e3bc-5a93-90b4-9f79a0997b62" :question/math-academy-id "q-9911" :question/problem "Find the possible values of the constant $k$ given that the matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & k \\\\ 1 & k & -k \\\\ 5 & k & k\\end{bmatrix}$ is singular.\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "First, let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 & k \\\\ 1 & k & -k \\\\ 5 & k & k\\end{vmatrix} & =1\\begin{vmatrix}k & -k \\\\ k & k\\end{vmatrix}-(-1)\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -k \\\\ 5 & k\\end{vmatrix}+k\\begin{vmatrix}1 & k \\\\ 5 & k\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =({k}^{2}+{k}^{2})+(k+5k)+k(k-5k) \\\\  & =2{k}^{2}+6k+k\\cdot (-4k) \\\\  & =2{k}^{2}+6k-4{k}^{2} \\\\  & =-2{k}^{2}+6k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the matrix is singular, the determinant is $0,$ and we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{k}^{2}+6k & =0 \\\\ k(-2k+6) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $k=0$ and $k=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362379 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28576cc0-5b53-52a1-993c-52e6981f0e23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361011} {:answer/id #uuid "41df7ae9-3272-5a1d-b2c6-8730c296899a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Any real number" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361129} {:answer/id #uuid "a0b997e7-f26c-5ca5-b635-2f16f0cab1c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361556} {:answer/id #uuid "a555d756-093c-5a66-ac31-9396b628afff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361577} {:answer/id #uuid "d8a9bece-ac6f-57f6-abed-e01a47669f57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361800}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186361556} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f3d836f-c871-5036-ada8-8e641e2d9862" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362089}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ec95474f-3d89-5428-8221-da33b63b3f3b" :question/math-academy-id "q-9961" :question/problem "Given that the determinant of  $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & n & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$ is $-6$, find the value of $n.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $n:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & n & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & =1\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-n\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}+0\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4-n\\cdot 2+0 \\\\  & =4-2n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the determinant of the matrix is $-6,$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-2n & =-6 \\\\ -2n & =-10 \\\\ n & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362496 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0368d3ef-7ff9-5da6-857d-96776c228a1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362498}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362498} :answer-field/id #uuid "47f7b160-4a23-58c9-94a1-d65bba0f866a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362497}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9d35a688-8b95-5b4b-a5d5-a0c9f931f061" :question/math-academy-id "q-167889" :question/problem "Water flows over the element $E$ according to the vector field \n\n$$\nF(x,y)=6y\\,i+x\\,j.\n$$\n\nSince the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $E$ found in part 5 has zero $k$-component, it may be considered a two-dimensional vector defined in terms of $i$ and $j$ only. Use this to find an expression for the flux of $F$ across the element in terms of $x$ and $y.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Flux = \n$$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{E}\n$$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\text{d}s.$" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a vector field and $E$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $E$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{E}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit vector to $E.$\n\n![](images/2c/2cd5e4c7ab86cdb999e239e986713a51c07b8decb629b1ee4a656077590f04bb.png)\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{E}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{E}(6y\\,i+x\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{x}{3}\\,i+\\frac{y}{3}\\,j)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{E}2xy+\\frac{1}{3}xy\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{E}\\frac{7}{3}xy\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362860 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7fea6d91-968c-5f4d-8499-0425dc5d7fe2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(3x+2){e}^{3x}}{9}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362862} {:answer/id #uuid "df58de38-7310-5f19-9844-03c75ef49b7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(6x+1){e}^{3x}}{9}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362863} {:answer/id #uuid "f721fccd-de41-549f-b75e-093592b0c032" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(6x+4){e}^{3x}}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362864} {:answer/id #uuid "7db9ef7b-e876-5195-b2bc-5e90b186c364" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(4x-3){e}^{3x}}{9}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362865} {:answer/id #uuid "cab8da55-0308-5e36-8634-1b58557947b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(4x-1){e}^{3x}}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362866}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362863} :answer-field/id #uuid "1bcef777-2395-5b4a-81aa-40676383c492" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362861}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "bd0da194-e627-59a2-b874-56e9ce11e475" :question/math-academy-id "q-114291" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int (2x+1){e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle \\int (2x+1){e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle \\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=2x+1\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=2 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{3x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int (2x+1)\\cdot {e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =(2x+1)\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{3x}}{3}-\\int \\frac{{e}^{3x}}{3}\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (2x+1){e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{(2x+1){e}^{3x}}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}\\int {e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{(2x+1){e}^{3x}}{3}-\\frac{2{e}^{3x}}{9}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{(6x+1){e}^{3x}}{9}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362867 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "364fe6cb-9d03-53ce-a165-42f5c54b95ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3e}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362869} {:answer/id #uuid "c21264c7-8089-52ce-9a48-6dad38c7f2ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362870} {:answer/id #uuid "ef9db4ec-3467-518d-a30c-e4d04ca7e957" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{e}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362871} {:answer/id #uuid "b4b710cd-dff5-5c54-9db5-d82e41461d08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362872} {:answer/id #uuid "c0fe8a21-d6a2-507f-9611-cdd51fb00208" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362873}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362873} :answer-field/id #uuid "08dd7829-9567-51b3-9a69-757f02b76b0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362868}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "65eb7d35-cf19-5d83-87df-2f6cd4fcb07e" :question/math-academy-id "q-114317" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1/4}x{e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{4x} & ⇒ & v=\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot {e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{4x}}{4}-\\int \\left(\\frac{{e}^{4x}}{4}\\right)\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1/4}x{e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\frac{1}{4}x{e}^{4x}|}_{0}^{1/4}-\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1/4}{e}^{4x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{1}{4}x{e}^{4x}|}_{0}^{1/4}-{\\frac{1}{16}{e}^{4x}|}_{0}^{1/4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{4(1/4)}-0\\cdot {e}^{4\\cdot 0}]-\\frac{1}{16}[{e}^{4(1/4)}-{e}^{4\\cdot 0}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\frac{e}{4}-0]-\\frac{1}{16}[e-1] \\\\  & =\\frac{e}{16}-\\frac{e}{16}+\\frac{1}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362996 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ba8cff6-a436-587b-947b-b845d40cdf1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x{e}^{2x+5}}{4}+\\frac{3{e}^{2x+5}}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362998} {:answer/id #uuid "0bf7139d-5b6a-513e-8c05-ea38b19064dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3{e}^{2x}}{2}-\\frac{3x{e}^{2x}}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362999} {:answer/id #uuid "1a22e02f-c34e-55ad-b5ee-acd29d97566a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3{e}^{2x+5}}{2}-\\frac{3x{e}^{2x+5}}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363000} {:answer/id #uuid "b3c30e70-a737-5f41-9c71-a8d0f2188c7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3x{e}^{2x+5}}{2}-\\frac{3{e}^{2x+5}}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363001} {:answer/id #uuid "b1a00544-29af-5a0e-9dbd-8c18e64ae108" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2x+5}}{2}+\\frac{3{e}^{2x+5}}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363002}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363001} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4e46148-8029-5a37-a794-c93ccb0b0139" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362997}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "708f4971-ac44-5610-9dca-92afa6cd2300" :question/math-academy-id "q-12558" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int 3x{e}^{2x+5}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle \\int 3x{e}^{2x+5}\\text{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle \\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=3x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=3 \\\\ {v}^{′}={e}^{2x+5}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\frac{{e}^{2x+5}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 3x\\cdot {e}^{2x+5}\\,\\text{d}x & =3x\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{2x+5}}{2}-\\int \\frac{{e}^{2x+5}}{2}\\cdot 3\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3x{e}^{2x+5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3x{e}^{2x+5}}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}\\int {e}^{2x+5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3x{e}^{2x+5}}{2}-\\frac{3{e}^{2x+5}}{4}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363003 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9775c1a1-be1f-5d4a-8b38-2b22b3877645" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+5)}{3}+\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+5)}{9}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363005} {:answer/id #uuid "25af2b05-29e6-557b-85e3-77fd7abe9e44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+5)}{3}+\\frac{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+5)}{9}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363006} {:answer/id #uuid "91cb4a25-ae24-57be-8930-89439bfcd808" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+5)}{3}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+5)}{9}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363007} {:answer/id #uuid "e1b7d50e-bf4c-5d3c-8f65-093e0d804de6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+5)}{9}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+5)}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363008} {:answer/id #uuid "b9c244ca-ce7e-5f14-8b4e-fc2f90fec15c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+5)}{9}+\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+5)}{9}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363009}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363007} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d5a6fdb-0204-5d6b-854a-5929492322cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363004}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6d889fae-ff07-5704-acc9-b1fed02f79be" :question/math-academy-id "q-12565" :question/problem "Calculate  $$\n\\displaystyle \\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+5)\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle \\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+5)\\text{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle \\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+5)\\, & ⟹\\,v=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+5)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe, substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+5)\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+5)}{3}\\right)-\\int \\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+5)}{3}\\right)\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+5)\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+5)}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+5)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+5)}{3}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+5)}{9}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363031 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29f3795a-9c5d-564b-99c5-8234a8f8adae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363033} {:answer/id #uuid "17117ea5-c48d-5a75-87dd-44fd4b306516" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "i-2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363034} {:answer/id #uuid "a1a1d040-84f6-5093-b8b7-a02c512a3ef2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\,i-2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363035} {:answer/id #uuid "afb47ff5-03cb-5158-9e15-6ff6eeca5436" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363036} {:answer/id #uuid "0c788786-b66b-5944-b4ad-21c56064f8a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,i+3\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363037}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363037} :answer-field/id #uuid "d07c4d10-4f15-5f81-9aca-9c05aa1a4821" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363032}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "55fb49e6-3301-5747-997a-6aea4f919a61" :question/math-academy-id "q-130201" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $xy=3-{x}^{2}-{y}^{3}.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(-1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xy & =3-{x}^{2}-{y}^{3} \\\\ xy+{x}^{2}+{y}^{3} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=xy+{x}^{2}+{y}^{3},$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=3.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(-1,-1)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(-1,-1)={f}_{y}(-1,-1)i-{f}_{x}(-1,-1)j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(-1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy+{x}^{2}+{y}^{3})i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy+{x}^{2}+{y}^{3})j \\\\  & =(x+3{y}^{2})i-(y+2x)j \\\\ t(-1,-1) & =(-1+3(-1)^{2})i-(-1+2(-1))j \\\\  & =2\\,i+3\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $2\\,i+3\\,j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364275 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31cdfcf9-98d2-5183-877d-c6911c5786e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364277} {:answer/id #uuid "0c65c166-229d-5e3b-933e-08d975e562bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364278} {:answer/id #uuid "aa2cde5a-f869-5481-8dc3-00dd3ad08d94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364279} {:answer/id #uuid "a7d225f4-6349-507e-b1a7-f6934a27b132" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{23\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364280} {:answer/id #uuid "23f19c4b-e2a1-55d9-989d-819c4ed4c37f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364281}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364279} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce18f523-92fa-512f-8b3f-d199baff422a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364276}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6d40fd3d-7b15-59ca-8ee5-88d3927298a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-49678" :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }12x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's use integration by parts. We set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=12x & ⇒ & {u}^{′}=12 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & ⇒ & v=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 12x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =12x\\cdot (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))\\cdot 12\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }12x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-12x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x){|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }+12{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-12x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x){|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }+12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x){|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-12[\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\pi )] \\\\  & +12[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi )] \\\\  & =-12[-\\pi -\\pi ]+12[0-0] \\\\  & =-12[-2\\pi ] \\\\  & =24\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364309 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b63d502-5d59-57aa-8402-34f229d7c402" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364311} {:answer/id #uuid "d929314d-5095-5284-a04d-ed11a462fa9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "i-3\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364312} {:answer/id #uuid "47b22ede-a70a-5c2c-93d8-02196b170ae3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\,i-2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364313} {:answer/id #uuid "18da3830-6440-532f-9cfa-3f5d7f38caec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,i+j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364314} {:answer/id #uuid "41a6476e-7908-5df0-9348-8c27d17118c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,i+3\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364315}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364312} :answer-field/id #uuid "727e4de1-593d-5c99-aa03-6535fb642416" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364310}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ddd3bf49-0b80-5ad0-bb5a-ddf4f0c0659f" :question/math-academy-id "q-72388" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $x+1={x}^{2}y.$ Find a tangent vector to that curve at the point $(1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & ={x}^{2}y \\\\ {x}^{2}y-x-1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y-x-1$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,2)$ can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(1,2)={f}_{y}(1,2)i-{f}_{x}(1,2)j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y-x-1)i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y-x-1)j \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\,i-(2xy-1)j \\\\ t(1,2) & =(1)^{2}\\,i-(2(1)(2)-1)j \\\\  & =i-3\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our tangent vector is $i-3\\,j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364316 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b4b0f044-128c-5f1b-aa69-2500dbaf5ea2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(0,-1)$ and $(0,-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364318} {:answer/id #uuid "bdd3ddf3-f39a-5e23-9542-809cca8045dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(0,0)$ and $(-1,-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364319} {:answer/id #uuid "cd3c3702-cba3-57ad-b3e0-2c994913bd41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(0,3)$ and $(0,-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364320} {:answer/id #uuid "71ef2f17-55ba-507e-8bbc-fc7b6bfebfc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(3,0)$ and $(-1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364321} {:answer/id #uuid "9e254c1d-c4f8-51bc-9734-35d90904ed56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(0,1)$ and $(1,-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364322}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364319} :answer-field/id #uuid "e462412c-3cbc-5541-9147-125fc8a64b29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364317}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "32727d15-71dd-5c57-93a9-b5f15381c7c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-72416" :question/problem "At which points on the curve $2xy-3{x}^{2}=-y$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $j?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2xy-3{x}^{2}=-y \\\\ 2xy-3{x}^{2}+y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=2xy-3{x}^{2}+y$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy-3{x}^{2}+y)i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy-3{x}^{2}+y)j \\\\  & =(2x+1)\\,i-2(y-3x)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $j$, we must have $t\\cdot j=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot j & =0 \\\\ ((2x+1)\\,i-2(y-3x)\\,j)\\cdot j & =0 \\\\ -2(y-3x) & =0 \\\\ y & =3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=3x$ into the equation of our curve gives:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2xy-3{x}^{2}+y & =0 \\\\ 2x(3x)-3{x}^{2}+3x & =0 \\\\ 6{x}^{2}-3{x}^{2}+3x & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+3x & =0 \\\\ 3x(x+1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0\\,\\text{or}\\,x=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally:\n\n- If $x=0$, we get $y=0$ and the corresponding point is $(0,0).$\n\n- If $x=-1$, we get $y=-3$ and the corresponding point is $(-1,-3).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364948 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b194044-2804-5fcf-a8b9-85153f639110" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364950} {:answer/id #uuid "54638e37-8e75-56d4-9a53-7e2c86da678b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364951} {:answer/id #uuid "c81acd3d-ac95-59d4-82b7-c347ecf84382" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364952} {:answer/id #uuid "0c9c5b60-f770-5f9a-ba14-7c25357899e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364953} {:answer/id #uuid "00b39530-0e0b-51c1-a111-9de7ad7bbe3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364954}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364953} :answer-field/id #uuid "edaea1a1-bae4-5261-82aa-d4974642b5d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364949}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "16690ec9-e7e6-5efe-838c-d6c519315582" :question/math-academy-id "q-128308" :question/problem "Find the value of $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}7 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} & =7\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-1\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}-3\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =7(6-1)-1(0+3)-3(0+9) \\\\  & =7\\cdot 5-1\\cdot 3-3\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =35-3-27 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365547 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40e1e46e-bc20-5e49-8f3c-e2b13ee9e67f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{67}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365549}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365549} :answer-field/id #uuid "20261a72-2d87-53f3-be96-928a95530e46" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365548}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fbb701de-fecd-5033-b2e0-5f19c68c7b4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-280402" :question/problem "Given that $B=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & k+2 & -5 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$ and $det(B)=70$, find the value of $k.$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(B) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & k+2 & -5 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\begin{vmatrix}k+2 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 6\\end{vmatrix}-0\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -5 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{vmatrix}+2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & k+2 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3(6\\cdot (k+2)-(-5)(-1))+2(1\\cdot (-1)-4\\cdot (k+2)) \\\\  & =3(6k+12-5)+2(-1-4k-8) \\\\  & =3(6k+7)+2(-4k-9) \\\\  & =18k+21-8k-18 \\\\  & =10k+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the determinant of the matrix is $70,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10k+3 & =70 \\\\ 10k & =67 \\\\ k & =\\frac{67}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365550 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e337463-b3e6-57c5-a2b0-3b2f8be1327c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365552}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365552} :answer-field/id #uuid "113f8edf-ce63-5e3c-8e20-25df8fcc000b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365551} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5aa7c3b-3cc6-5915-9f4e-4ad87619a68f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365554}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365554} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ae0cbef-0d94-5f00-b36b-22a479b003a4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365553}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7046d61a-2100-5f6e-80e3-9459516462dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-280543" :question/problem "Find, in **ascending order**, the values ​​of $x$ for which the following matrix is singular.\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}x+2 & 2x & 4 \\\\ 3 & x & x+1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIn ascending order, $x=$\n{{field-1}} and $x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's use our formula to find an expression for the determinant in terms of $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}x+2 & 2x & 4 \\\\ 3 & x & x+1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & =(x+2)\\begin{vmatrix}x & x+1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}-2x\\begin{vmatrix}3 & x+1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}+4\\begin{vmatrix}3 & x \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(x+2)(3x)-2x(8-x)+4(-x) \\\\  & =3x(x+2)-2x(8-x)-4x \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}+6x-16x+2{x}^{2}-4x \\\\  & =5{x}^{2}-14x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the matrix is singular, the determinant is $0,$ and we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{2}-14x & =0 \\\\ x(5x-14) & =0 \\\\ x & =0\\,\\text{or}\\,x=\\frac{14}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{14}{5}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366794 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c502c08e-cbb1-5442-bcb9-424b6777ac3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438664} {:answer/id #uuid "003139e8-881a-52b3-9ea9-221baca3d942" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438665}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186438664} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee174a06-895c-5583-812c-7c4468fa2511" :answer-field/key "$det(D)=1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439361} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4493f073-130a-5643-b440-425f3034460d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438666} {:answer/id #uuid "0508c95e-9b72-5cb3-8464-ae610809503a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438667}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186438667} :answer-field/id #uuid "495bc1a5-e85e-5043-b4d4-3fe8b15554ed" :answer-field/key "$D$ is a singular matrix" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439362} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1502369-e88d-503e-90f4-0f6ab218c61c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438668} {:answer/id #uuid "516ece6f-6106-541c-abfc-8c583469231d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438669}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186438669} :answer-field/id #uuid "e222facc-8617-59fb-b0f3-b2ffe6c181c7" :answer-field/key "$det(D)=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439363}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2e3acb77-06f5-5592-8e02-3140d23a412e" :question/math-academy-id "q-9835" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $D$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 6 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 6 & 7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 5 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{vmatrix}-2\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 7\\end{vmatrix}+3\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(28-30)-2(7-5)+3(6-4) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-2)-2\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-2-4+6 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $det(D)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements “$D$ is a singular matrix” and $det(D)=0$ are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367603 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b416a21d-f860-5ddd-9887-3909cbcfc268" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{4}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367605} {:answer/id #uuid "84ff0b6d-4bc8-58b4-8c1f-7733a5d10ac0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367606} {:answer/id #uuid "8e61b06f-5796-5daf-a560-740f317dd6f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)}{4}+\\frac{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367607} {:answer/id #uuid "52c4db08-6d75-5662-ba65-98e14935cb32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{4}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367608} {:answer/id #uuid "665ef108-98da-5b42-b5b6-51f12caa5073" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{4}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367609}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367606} :answer-field/id #uuid "7906d722-793f-554d-8af3-30e86eebaf11" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186367604}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c948087e-b394-5663-8b66-c2d6a56d25e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-14451" :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int 2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle \\int 2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\text{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle \\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=2x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=2 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\, & ⟹\\,v=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 2x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =2x\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}\\right)-\\int \\left(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{2}\\right)\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369182 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81223159-c5d4-588d-9314-66a3a430542a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{6}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369184} {:answer/id #uuid "b8e66f76-6ad4-5bdc-abf7-bebed8056693" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369185} {:answer/id #uuid "634bfffa-5e14-55f7-a686-f6fb8d6cec2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369186} {:answer/id #uuid "e31e6823-8cbb-53ae-bb81-764ac833481b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369187} {:answer/id #uuid "99c6a89a-45a4-5739-bf2f-f0c0f3684523" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{12}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{6}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369188}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369185} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4e6da79-a939-5e5c-a0d3-eb489cc25e8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369183}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5b02d1c4-7d7d-53ea-825a-e7c0df24295a" :question/math-academy-id "q-115108" :question/problem "![](images/93/93eeedaad213d620382392235ac91835106dd772892c8c3e4ec74e808ae22179.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=6$ and the vertical radius has length $b=3.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{6}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369258 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a914125-7099-5b0c-a1c1-9224eef57080" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369260} {:answer/id #uuid "7213e0d0-98dc-5715-bb95-024708c2eadc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369261} {:answer/id #uuid "9a304234-899b-5867-ae14-f933577257a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-12{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369262} {:answer/id #uuid "5c0a400c-fbad-505f-8344-98b05be346a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369263} {:answer/id #uuid "ce5940aa-094f-59da-a9fc-f4dda58c537a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-12{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369264}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369262} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc543956-16a6-524a-b735-fabf30c10bc9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369259}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "55218946-84ed-5872-a1c7-f6fd7a57a20f" :question/math-academy-id "q-124226" :question/problem "![](images/f4/f402fe97e6f76cd94b94331e7f30ddb09c978c93b86ce112a5232042a2f741cc.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}2y\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along the ellipse $9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=36,$ traversed from the point $(2,0)$ to the point $(-2,0),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.$*" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=2y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-12{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
